<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.11.5: Using Relationship Models to Answer Questions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01450</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.11.5: Using Relationship Models to Answer Questions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e81828f6-2f20-4e74-85c8-ae1d7e54cbaf</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para>A ball is dropped from a certain height. The table shows the rebound heights of the ball after a series of bounces.
</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>Bounce Number</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Height in Centimeters</emphasis></entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        30
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        6
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>From what height, approximately, do you think the ball was dropped? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>Between 150 cm and 180 cm. The rebound factors are \(\frac15\), \(\frac16\), and 0 (this last measurement is
    probably not reliable because it could have been a very small bounce height, which would be difficult to measure).
    Because \(\frac15\) of 150 is 30 and \(\frac16\) of 180 is 30, the ball was probably dropped from between 150 cm and
    180 cm.</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
